Skip the idle process in pgrep and pkill on Windows.
On Windows PID 0 is a real process, the idle process "[System
Process]", and it comes first in the process list. A pkill pattern
that matched it, even one as short as "ss" or "o", or -v with most
patterns, called kill(0), which signals the caller and its
descendants: pkill signalled itself in place of the idle process, and
unless the signal was 0 it ended there without reaching the processes
after it. pgrep listed PID 0, but exited 1 when that was its only
match, as a matched PID of 0 reads as no match.

Leave PID 0 out, as the process cannot be signalled anyway.

---
 procps/pgrep.c |    6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

--- a/procps/pgrep.c
+++ b/procps/pgrep.c
@@ -221,6 +221,12 @@
 
 		if (proc->pid == pid)
 			continue;
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+		/* Skip the idle process: kill(0) would signal pkill itself,
+		 * and a PID of 0 reads as no match */
+		if (proc->pid == 0)
+			continue;
+#endif
 
 		if (!OPT_INVERT) {
 			/* Quickly reject -sN -PN mismatches... unless -v */
